MODULE SELECTION RULE (deterministic)
1. selectedGroups = the groups of the user's selected domain tags. Candidates = the module questions whose domain equals the selected domain.
2. For each candidate: directHits = number of its tags that are selected; groupHits = number of its groups that are in selectedGroups; priority = 3 x directHits + groupHits.
3. Pick 6 greedily. At each step choose the candidate that (a) covers the most selected groups not yet covered by earlier picks, then (b) has the highest priority, then (c) covers the competencies that are least covered so far (counting the 4 shared questions and earlier picks), then (d) has the alphabetically first id. Never repeat a question.
4. If fewer than 6 candidates have priority above 0, fill the rest in the same sorted order.
5. AI selection (only if at least one AI tag is selected): apply the same greedy rule to the AI questions, using the selected AI tags and AI groups, and pick 3.
6. The same domain and tags must always return the same questions in the same order. Only the display order of options is random.

=====================================================
PART 7: SCORING ENGINE (PURE LOOKUP, NO AI)
=====================================================

Create src/track3/scoring.ts as pure functions with no network calls and no randomness.

1. Question score = the score of the chosen option (1 to 4).
2. Competency score = the mean of the scores of the shared and module questions (NOT the AI questions) that list that competency, rounded to 1 decimal. Evidence strength: 1 contributing question = "low", 2 to 3 = "medium", 4 or more = "high".
3. Group score = the mean of the scores of the session's module questions whose groups include that group, rounded to 1 decimal. Evidence strength as above. AI groups are scored the same way from the AI questions.
4. Tag status for every selected tag:
   - "Assessed directly" if a session question carries that tag; show the mean score of the questions carrying it.
   - "Assessed at group level" if no session question carries it but its group has a session question; show the group score, labelled "group score".
   - "Not assessed" otherwise.
   Tool tags (kind "tool") are never scored on their own. Show them as "Listed (tool), assessed through [group]" or "Listed (tool), not assessed".
5. weightedAverage = sum of (domain weight x competency score) over the 7 competencies.
6. Overall readiness = round(clamp((weightedAverage - 2) / 2, 0, 1) x 100). Reason: random guessing averages about 2.5, which maps to about 25, so the scale rewards only above-chance performance.
7. Labels: 0 to 34 "Early stage"; 35 to 54 "Developing"; 55 to 74 "Job-ready"; 75 to 100 "Strong". These cut-offs are provisional.
8. AI score (only if AI tags were selected): aiMean = mean of the AI question scores; aiReadiness = round(clamp((aiMean - 2) / 2, 0, 1) x 100). AI questions never change the overall readiness or the competency scores. Compare aiMean with the same experience bars (PART 8).
9. Strengths = up to 3 competencies at or above the strong bar, highest first. Gaps = up to 3 competencies below the expected bar, lowest first. If there are no gaps, show the lowest-scoring competency as "Next to build".
10. Missed-question review: list up to 3 questions across the whole session (including AI) where the chosen option scored 2 or less, lowest first. For each, show the question, the user's answer, and "A stronger approach:" followed by the text of the option that scored 4. Do not reveal the scores of the other options.

